# Name: MSX International
#
# MSX "International" character set (the European/Western variant -
# MSX also has distinct Japanese/Arabic/etc. regional ROM variants not
# covered here), bytes 0x80-0xFF - accented Latin letters, Greek
# letters, box/block-drawing graphics, math symbols. 0x00-0x7F is plain
# ASCII (unlisted below, kept as identity); entirely above the 7-bit
# ASCII range, so this carries none of CP437/ATASCII's VT100-
# control-code conflict risk (see TODO.md Abschnitt 6.3).
#
# Source: Wikipedia "MSX character set" (raw table, "International"
# variant specifically - there are several regional tables on that page).
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MSX_character_set
#
# Two positions (0xB4/0xB5, O-with-tilde) are noted by the source itself
# as ambiguous across MSX ROM revisions (could instead be O-with-double-
# acute) - the table's primary/first-listed value is used here, same
# approach as the built-in Atari ST charset's own documented homoglyphs.
#
# Deliberately NOT included:
# - 0xFF: the source's table marks this as the on-screen cursor
#   indicator, not a static character at all.
# - 21 other positions across 0xC0-0xD7 whose source table entry is a
#   real, distinct glyph, but every one of those specific code points
#   lies in Unicode's "Symbols for Legacy Computing" block (added in
#   Unicode 13.0+) and is above 0xFFFF - this file format has no
#   astral/surrogate-pair support at all (see komportcharset.cpp's
#   loadCustomCharsetFile(), "no surrogate-pair/astral support").
